= Introduction
<introduction>
Embodied-cognition accounts treat the mind as built on, and shaped by, the sensorimotor exchanges between a body and its environment @Glenberg2010@Barsalou2008@barsalou2008grounded. One component of that exchange is #emph[peripersonal space] (PPS), the multisensory region immediately surrounding the body within which incoming signals are integrated and prepared for action @rizzolatti1997space@diPellegrino2015@Serino2019@noel_2021. Audio--tactile and visuo--tactile paradigms measure tactile responses at different points along an approaching or receding stimulus trajectory @canzoneri2012dynamic@teneggi2013social. The resulting response gradient is often fitted with a sigmoid; when its shape and parameters are supported by the data, the midpoint and slope can describe the transition's location and sharpness @Bufacchi2018@Basile2024@Serino2019. Auditory--tactile reaction-time gradients can also reflect temporal expectancy and other design features, so a fitted curve alone does not establish a spatial PPS boundary @holmes2020sounds. On current accounts, PPS is not a distinct cortical module but a case of multisensory integration operating under body-centred coordinate frames with an additional proximity-gating term @noel_2018@bertoni_2025@straka2022impact, and the same electrophysiological signature has been linked to self-referenced consciousness states in sleep and in disorders of consciousness @bertoni_2026. A well-supported response curve can therefore offer a behavioural estimate of a transition along the probed trajectory. The remainder of this introduction traces a single argument in five steps. First, plasticity findings indicate that behavioural PPS estimates can follow the brain's body estimate rather than the physical body @noel_2021@Serino2019. Second, interoceptive signals belong in that estimate, yet the channel by which they reach it is unspecified @Ardizzi2018@salvato_2020@bogdanova_2021. Third, respiration is a plausible channel @saltafossi_2025@allen_2021. Fourth, the direct empirical test and the corresponding plasticity manipulation are absent from the published literature @spaccasassi_2019@karakoc_2025. Finally, the present study tests both.

== Peripersonal space is plastic and tracks the state of the body
<peripersonal-space-is-plastic-and-tracks-the-state-of-the-body>
The PPS sigmoid is malleable @Serino2019@Bufacchi2018@noel_2021@karakoc_2025. Tool use extends it to incorporate the tool @iriki1996coding@maravita2004tools; cooperative social interaction merges self and partner space @teneggi2013social; body-swap and full-body illusions relocate PPS with the illusorily perceived self-location @noel2015peripersonal@noel2015fullbody; and vestibular stimulation reshapes it in line with altered spatial orientation @pfeiffer2018vestibular. A systematic review of immersive-VR manipulations of PPS reports a comparable pattern. Across tool-use, social, and embodiment paradigms, the boundary tracks the multisensorially constructed body estimate that the VR display engages rather than the physical body @karakoc_2025. Read together, these manipulations are consistent with the view that PPS is anchored to the multisensorially estimated state of the body rather than to the physical body per se @blanke2015behavioral@Grivaz2017@serino2015bodypart@noel_2021. If PPS follows the brain's ongoing body estimate, then any signal that feeds that estimate, including afferents arising from inside the body, should in principle also shape PPS. The next subsection turns to evidence that interoceptive signals do.

== Interoception and the softening of the self--world boundary
<interoception-and-the-softening-of-the-selfworld-boundary>
A more recent and smaller literature extends this logic from exteroceptive to interoceptive signals. #cite(<Ardizzi2018>, form: "prose") reported that individuals with higher cardiac interoceptive accuracy have #emph[narrower] PPS boundaries, with the effect moderated by a dispositional tendency to attend to internal sensations. Brief focused-attention meditation has been reported to reduce the sharpness of the PPS sigmoid without contracting its extent @Chiarella2024; guided meditation has been reported to abolish the near--far attentional enhancement of auditory evoked responses in non-meditators @nguyen_2020; mindfulness training has been associated with looser perceived body boundaries and a shift toward allocentric spatial frames @hanley_2020; and sensory-attenuation contexts such as Floatation-REST have been associated with body-boundary dissolution that mediates anxiety reduction @hruby_2024. A kernel-density meta-analysis of neuroimaging studies locates the anatomical convergence between interoceptive and exteroceptive bodily-self processing in bilateral supramarginal gyrus and right-lateralised peri-central cortex, that is, in PPS-relevant parietal territory @salvato_2020. Considered together with the broader literature on breath, heart, and gut as contributors to self-experience @seth2013interoceptive@barrett2016theory@Park2019@bogdanova_2021, these findings are consistent with treating PPS as an #emph[intero--exteroceptive interface];, a zone in which interoceptive priors about the body meet the statistics of the surrounding world @salvato_2020@bogdanova_2021@noel_2021. What remains underspecified is #emph[which] interoceptive signals reach the PPS computation, and through which temporal channel.

== Respiration as an organiser of perception and multisensory integration
<respiration-as-an-organiser-of-perception-and-multisensory-integration>
Among interoceptive channels, breath is one practical candidate. Unlike other autonomic rhythms, respiration is both voluntarily controllable and automatically driven, and it carries chemoreceptor, mechanoreceptor, and proprioceptive afferents that converge on brainstem, insular, and limbic targets @Chan2024@Varga2017@heck2017breathing@Zelano2022. Evidence accumulated over the past decade indicates that respiratory phase modulates cortical excitability and perception beyond the olfactory system. Nasal inhalation has been associated with entrained oscillations in piriform cortex, amygdala, and hippocampus, and with speeded fearful-face discrimination and episodic retrieval @zelano2016nasal@mizuhara_2023. Respiration has been linked to posterior alpha power and to visual detection sensitivity, with excitability changes preceding performance fluctuations @kluger_2021. Tactile detection has been reported to peak in the first quadrant after expiration onset, and participants phase-lock their breathing to expected stimuli @grund_2021. Breath--heart coupling via respiratory sinus arrhythmia has been related to heartbeat-evoked potentials and to the gating of somatosensory input @Zaccaro2022@Heck2022. Across modalities, then, respiration appears to contribute to the temporal organisation of sensory processing rather than acting as a passive background state @Zelano2022@Heck2022.

Of direct relevance to the present work, #cite(<saltafossi_2025>, form: "prose") used the race-model inequality to provide evidence that respiratory phase modulates multisensory integration. Audio--tactile and audio--visual integration peaked at the expiration-to-inspiration transition, with multisensory gain dissociating from unimodal speed. Predictive-coding accounts formulate this by casting respiration as a temporal scaffold along which the brain allocates precision to competing sources of evidence about the body and the surrounding world, with breath phase modulating local and global neural gain across interoceptive and exteroceptive channels @allen_2021@braendholt_2023@engelen_2023. Because PPS can be characterised as multisensory integration operating under body-centred reference frames with an additional proximity-gating term @noel_2018@bertoni_2025@noel_2021, the respiratory-phase organisation that has been observed for generic multisensory integration may, in principle, also be observable in the PPS sigmoid. The next subsection sets out why this prediction has not yet been tested, and why a second, paradigmatic gap accompanies it.

== A missing test and a missing manipulation
<a-missing-test-and-a-missing-manipulation>
Two literatures have approached the same question without directly addressing it. PPS is a body-referenced multisensory integration process that has been shown to be shaped by interoceptive signals @Ardizzi2018@salvato_2020@bogdanova_2021, and respiratory phase has been reported to modulate perception and multisensory integration more generally @saltafossi_2025@kluger_2021@grund_2021. To our knowledge, no published study has delivered PPS-task stimuli at controlled respiratory phases and fit the sigmoid separately under each, and recent reviews of PPS computational models and of immersive-VR PPS paradigms describe no such design @bertoni_2025@karakoc_2025. The one empirical study that embeds a breathing manipulation within a PPS paradigm used breathing as an anxiety-induction procedure rather than as a phase manipulation @spaccasassi_2019, and the "embreathment" illusion shows that breathing feedback on a seen avatar can shift corporeal awareness without engaging the PPS sigmoid directly @monti2020embreathment@Allard2017@Betka2020@Betka2022. The gap is therefore at the intersection of the two literatures. Respiratory phase has been shown to modulate generic multisensory integration, but not the body-referenced multisensory integration that PPS isolates.

Addressing this first gap invites a second, paradigmatic question. The plasticity manipulations that have informed PPS theory, including tool use, rubber-hand and full-body illusions, and audio--tactile far-and-near pairing, share the structural feature of introducing a synchronous multimodal signal that causal-inference accounts treat as evidence of a shared generative source @samad_2015@chancel_2021@costantini_2016@parise_2016. To our knowledge, no analogous manipulation has been reported for a purely visceral--exteroceptive pairing, and the systematic review of immersive-VR PPS paradigms catalogues tool-use, social, and embodiment variants without listing a bioresponsive physiological-coupling manipulation @karakoc_2025. The nearest precedent is cardiovisual. #cite(<sel_2016>, form: "prose") reported that a pulsing visual shade locked to the observer's heartbeat increased identification with an otherwise ambiguous self--other face and modulated heartbeat-evoked potentials, with the effect scaling with individual interoceptive accuracy. Bioresponsive virtual reality renders the corresponding experiment for the respiratory channel technically feasible. Visual elements of an immersive environment can be tied in real time to the participant's ongoing respiratory signal, an arrangement recently described as #emph[exteroceptive--interoceptive sensory substitution] @Goral2024. In the taxonomy of PPS-plasticity paradigms this corresponds to a bioresponsive environmental-coupling manipulation, and, to our knowledge, it has not yet been reported in the empirical record @karakoc_2025@serino2017peripersonal@belli2021breathink@Belli2024. The present study addresses both gaps in a single design.

== The present study
<the-present-study>
Participants perform an audio--tactile PPS task @canzoneri2012dynamic@teneggi2013social under instructed 4--4--4--4 box breathing, cycling through inhalation, post-inhalation hold, exhalation, and post-exhalation hold. Audio--tactile stimuli are scheduled during the two breath-hold phases, permitting separate SOA-response curves for post-inhalation and post-exhalation holds under the assumption of compliant breathing. Participants complete this task under two VR conditions with both orders represented in the pooled cohort. In the #emph[Viscereality] condition, an ovular orb in front of the participant expands and contracts in real time with their breathing, providing a visual environment whose spatial dynamics are coupled to the breath signal @Goral2024. In the #emph[Control] condition, the same task is performed against a dark field containing only the central breath-performance indicator that scales with respiratory excursion, providing minimal visual feedback for breath performance without spatial coupling.

The preregistered main prediction was that, if coupling an exteroceptive spatial signal to the interoceptive breath signal engages processes linked to body ownership and PPS plasticity @samad_2015@chancel_2021@Ardizzi2018, the breath-coupled condition would show a larger PPS response during post-inhalation than post-exhalation holds, yielding a Condition $times$ Phase $times$ SOA interaction. A secondary Control-only Phase $times$ SOA test asked whether scheduled respiratory phase modulated the SOA response without spatial visual coupling @saltafossi_2025@kluger_2021@grund_2021. The registered parameter predictions concerned the sigmoid midpoint interaction and a less sharp boundary under Viscereality, conditional on interpretable sigmoid fits @Chiarella2024@hanley_2020@hruby_2024@nguyen_2020. Trait interoceptive awareness (MAIA-2) and pictographic measures of perceived body boundaries, spatial frame of reference, and small-self experience were exploratory correlates rather than additional confirmed tests.
